1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,722
Every systematic review rests on a number
that almost never gets written down.

2
00:00:05,762 --> 00:00:06,904
The denominator.

3
00:00:06,944 --> 00:00:11,376
The set of papers that were
eligible before screening began.

4
00:00:11,976 --> 00:00:14,687
In practice that set is rebuilt by hand,

5
00:00:14,727 --> 00:00:17,576
from portals and interfaces whose coverage

6
00:00:17,616 --> 00:00:20,465
and query semantics change underneath you.

7
00:00:20,505 --> 00:00:25,320
Two people follow the same protocol
and end up with different corpora.

8
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:28,460
TopVenues makes that
denominator an object.

9
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:30,740
Declared, frozen, hashed, and citable.

10
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:33,240
This is what working with one looks like.

11
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:36,561
Installation is a clone and one script.

12
00:00:36,601 --> 00:00:40,170
Nothing is fetched from a
publisher at review time,

13
00:00:40,210 --> 00:00:43,608
and nothing depends on
a service staying online.

14
00:00:44,208 --> 00:00:49,686
The script builds an isolated environment
from pinned, hash-checked dependencies,

15
00:00:49,726 --> 00:00:53,432
then materializes the corpus
from a compressed snapshot

16
00:00:53,472 --> 00:00:55,584
that ships with the repository.

17
00:00:56,184 --> 00:00:58,685
Then it verifies rather than asserts.

18
00:00:58,725 --> 00:01:02,463
The snapshot's SHA-256 is
checked against the manifest,

19
00:01:02,503 --> 00:01:07,064
the record counts are checked
against the numbers the paper prints,

20
00:01:07,104 --> 00:01:09,096
and the full test suite runs.

21
00:01:09,696 --> 00:01:13,094
Twenty thousand three
hundred and five records.

22
00:01:13,134 --> 00:01:18,727
Seventeen thousand four hundred and
ninety-one abstracts. Every test passing.

23
00:01:18,767 --> 00:01:23,376
A reviewer reaches this state
without asking anyone for access.

24
00:01:23,976 --> 00:01:26,909
The interface opens on
what you are holding.

25
00:01:26,949 --> 00:01:32,584
Fourteen thousand eight hundred and fifty-nine
papers across twenty declared venues,

26
00:01:32,624 --> 00:01:38,232
ninety-four percent carrying a searchable
abstract, and every record ready to cite.

27
00:01:38,832 --> 00:01:40,870
Nothing here is a live query.

28
00:01:40,910 --> 00:01:44,166
The venue list is a
declared scientific scope,

29
00:01:44,206 --> 00:01:48,648
and changing it is a release
decision, not a filter you slide.

30
00:01:49,248 --> 00:01:51,721
Start with an ordinary question.

31
00:01:51,761 --> 00:01:57,140
Ranked search orders title, abstract
and author matches by relevance,

32
00:01:57,180 --> 00:02:00,792
over the whole frozen
corpus, in milliseconds.

33
00:02:01,392 --> 00:02:07,231
Narrow it the way a protocol narrows: by
venue tier, by year, by author position.

34
00:02:07,271 --> 00:02:13,368
The count updates, and that count is the
number you will report as your denominator.

35
00:02:13,968 --> 00:02:17,221
Every row opens into the record behind it.

36
00:02:17,261 --> 00:02:23,808
The abstract that was matched, the venue,
the tier, the DOI, and the publisher link.

37
00:02:23,848 --> 00:02:27,768
You are reading the
evidence, not a summary of it.

38
00:02:28,368 --> 00:02:29,492
And it leaves.

39
00:02:29,532 --> 00:02:35,644
CSV for a screening sheet, JSON for
a pipeline, BibTeX for the manuscript,

40
00:02:35,684 --> 00:02:41,088
taken straight from DBLP's canonical
entries rather than retyped.

41
00:02:41,688 --> 00:02:45,687
Insights is where the
corpus stops being a list.

42
00:02:45,727 --> 00:02:50,568
It reads in four passes: what
it holds, how a topic moves,

43
00:02:50,608 --> 00:02:55,488
who produces that work, and
where it cannot answer at all.

44
00:02:56,088 --> 00:02:57,362
Composition first.

45
00:02:57,402 --> 00:03:00,939
Papers per venue, per
year, and per record class.

46
00:03:00,979 --> 00:03:04,880
Full articles are seventy-eight
percent of the corpus;

47
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:10,176
the rare classes are the ones a
protocol has to decide about explicitly.

48
00:03:10,776 --> 00:03:12,751
Every aggregate is a door.

49
00:03:12,791 --> 00:03:18,097
Click a bar and you are in Search,
filtered to exactly those records.

50
00:03:18,137 --> 00:03:21,624
No chart here is a
picture you have to trust.

51
00:03:22,224 --> 00:03:25,651
Now the pass that changes
how you read a field.

52
00:03:25,691 --> 00:03:29,044
Ask for a topic and you
get its yearly volume.

53
00:03:29,084 --> 00:03:33,396
Large language models: six
papers in two thousand nineteen,

54
00:03:33,436 --> 00:03:37,272
two hundred and seventy in
two thousand twenty-five.

55
00:03:37,872 --> 00:03:39,424
But the corpus grew too.

56
00:03:39,464 --> 00:03:43,072
So the second chart divides
by each year's corpus size.

57
00:03:43,112 --> 00:03:48,112
That is the difference between a topic
that is genuinely taking over a field

58
00:03:48,152 --> 00:03:51,336
and a topic that is merely
riding its expansion.

59
00:03:51,936 --> 00:03:55,360
And it names the venues
carrying that growth,

60
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:58,440
so the next question, where do I submit,

61
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:03,792
or where do I read, has an answer
grounded in the same frozen corpus.

62
00:04:04,392 --> 00:04:06,536
The fourth pass is people.

63
00:04:06,576 --> 00:04:13,004
Researcher Radar ranks who recurs, and
it is deliberate about what it is not:

64
00:04:13,044 --> 00:04:16,028
it measures presence in this corpus,

65
00:04:16,068 --> 00:04:20,184
never citations, quality,
seniority or authority.

66
00:04:20,784 --> 00:04:25,986
Three rankings answer three different
questions. Paper count finds volume.

67
00:04:26,026 --> 00:04:29,103
Tier weighting favours
the strongest venues.

68
00:04:29,143 --> 00:04:32,787
Top-four concentration asks
something else entirely:

69
00:04:32,827 --> 00:04:38,352
what share of an author's work lands at
the four venues that define the field.

70
00:04:38,952 --> 00:04:41,235
Authorship position asks a third.

71
00:04:41,275 --> 00:04:44,614
First author surfaces
the people doing the work;

72
00:04:44,654 --> 00:04:47,781
last author surfaces
the people directing it.

73
00:04:47,821 --> 00:04:53,664
For building a reference list, or a program
committee, those are not the same list.

74
00:04:54,264 --> 00:04:56,044
And no row is a verdict.

75
00:04:56,084 --> 00:05:00,593
Open one and you get the
trajectory behind it, year by year,

76
00:05:00,633 --> 00:05:05,825
split by byline position, with the
direct collaborations it rests on.

77
00:05:05,865 --> 00:05:08,064
The heuristic shows its work.

78
00:05:08,664 --> 00:05:11,208
Two derived views close the pass.

79
00:05:11,248 --> 00:05:14,809
Authors whose byline
moved from first to last,

80
00:05:14,849 --> 00:05:18,019
which is where new groups become visible.

81
00:05:18,059 --> 00:05:21,151
And authors whose annual rate is rising.

82
00:05:21,191 --> 00:05:26,280
Both are described as shortlists
to investigate, not as findings.

83
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:32,379
The fourth pass is the one most tools
omit: where the corpus cannot speak.

84
00:05:32,419 --> 00:05:36,721
Abstract coverage per venue,
published rather than hidden.

85
00:05:36,761 --> 00:05:42,600
ESORICS sits at six percent, because
Springer does not expose those abstracts.

86
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:48,846
That matters, because an abstract search
cannot include what it cannot read.

87
00:05:48,886 --> 00:05:52,063
A gap you can see is
an inclusion decision.

88
00:05:52,103 --> 00:05:56,592
A gap you cannot see is a silent
exclusion in your protocol.

89
00:05:57,192 --> 00:06:02,299
The Evidence page states what this
snapshot verifies and what it does not.

90
00:06:02,339 --> 00:06:04,664
Abstract quality was not asserted;

91
00:06:04,704 --> 00:06:07,793
it was measured, on a
venue-stratified sample

92
00:06:07,833 --> 00:06:10,824
of two hundred records
reviewed by a human.

93
00:06:11,424 --> 00:06:14,288
One hundred and sixty-nine were complete,

94
00:06:14,328 --> 00:06:17,545
uncontaminated, and
matched the sampled paper.

95
00:06:17,585 --> 00:06:19,741
Eighty-four and a half percent,

96
00:06:19,781 --> 00:06:23,069
with a ninety-five percent
Wilson interval from

97
00:06:23,109 --> 00:06:26,822
seventy-eight point eight
to eighty-eight point nine.

98
00:06:26,862 --> 00:06:30,120
The uncertainty is
reported, not rounded away.

99
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:34,640
And the release cannot be
edited from the interface.

100
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:38,066
Refreshing creates a
named successor profile;

101
00:06:38,106 --> 00:06:42,102
the snapshot a paper cites
is never mutated in place.

102
00:06:42,142 --> 00:06:45,264
That is what makes a hash worth printing.

103
00:06:45,864 --> 00:06:50,434
So what does this buy a researcher
who already knows their field.

104
00:06:50,474 --> 00:06:53,808
Three things, and none
of them is a search box.

105
00:06:54,408 --> 00:06:57,794
A denominator you can put
in a paper, with a hash,

106
00:06:57,834 --> 00:07:01,426
that a reviewer can reproduce
offline in one command.

107
00:07:01,466 --> 00:07:05,606
The methods section stops being
a description of what you did

108
00:07:05,646 --> 00:07:08,592
and becomes something
someone else can run.

109
00:07:09,192 --> 00:07:11,282
A fixed corpus turns questions

110
00:07:11,322 --> 00:07:16,892
that used to take a week of manual
counting into questions you ask in a second.

111
00:07:16,932 --> 00:07:19,804
Topic dynamics normalized against growth.

112
00:07:19,844 --> 00:07:22,432
Author concentration at the top four.

113
00:07:22,472 --> 00:07:24,744
Byline movement across a decade.

114
00:07:25,344 --> 00:07:30,851
And every number on every screen
opens into the records underneath it.

115
00:07:30,891 --> 00:07:33,070
Nothing asks for your trust.

116
00:07:33,110 --> 00:07:37,944
That is the difference between
a dashboard and an instrument.

117
00:07:38,544 --> 00:07:39,377
TopVenues.

118
00:07:39,417 --> 00:07:44,353
Open source, MIT licensed,
with the corpus, the manifest,

119
00:07:44,393 --> 00:07:48,408
and the reproduction
script in one repository.
